---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HubPageShell from '../components/hub/HubPageShell.astro';

const nav = [
  { id: 'fitness', label: 'The right tool' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Questions this hub answers' },
  { id: 'methods', label: 'The methods' },
  { id: 'before', label: 'Before any method is sold' },
];

const pageDescription =
  'Foundation repair methods compared: helical piers vs push piers, micropiles, compaction grouting, foam jacking, and other deep foundations. A method name is not a diagnosis. Use the tool that matches the condition.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': ['Article', 'FAQPage'],
  headline: 'Repair Methods',
  description: pageDescription,
  url: 'https://www.myfoundationrepairconsultant.com/repair-methods',
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://www.myfoundationrepairconsultant.com/repair-methods',
  },
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'My Foundation Repair Consultant' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'My Foundation Repair Consultant',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/6983db0242b9b6302c661059/fa18c5f7-2e51-4ae1-81d8-0517c590d714/MFRC+Logo.jpg',
    },
  },
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between a helical pier and a push pier?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Helical piers are rotated into the ground using helical plates. Push piers are pressed from the existing foundation using the building weight as reaction. They are not the same tool and they are not interchangeable on every house.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'When is compaction grouting most effective?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'When the job is densifying suitable soil and improving support in that ground — not when the need is a structural pile, and not as a substitute for expanding foam under a slab.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'When is foam jacking the wrong tool?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'When the problem is an active water, erosion, or weak-soil condition, or when the need is pile underpinning of a footing rather than filling an accessible slab void.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is a quieter or less expensive method sometimes the right method?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. Soil, water, access, structure weight, scope, and cost can make a simpler method the honest answer if it matches the condition that was found.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does a crack mean a house needs piers?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No. A crack is a symptom. Drainage, plumbing leaks, seasonal movement, and material behavior can produce cracks without a deep-foundation repair.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Repair Methods"
  description={pageDescription}
  hub={true}
  quote="The useful question is not which repair method sounds strongest. It is what problem has actually been identified, and whether the proposed method is designed to solve that problem."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <HubPageShell
    title="Repair Methods"
    subhead="A method name is not a diagnosis"
    description="This guide is a starting place for names that show up in a proposal. Read the questions first if you want the short path. Open a method card when you need the tool itself."
    glance={[
      { label: 'Start here', value: 'What problem is being solved?' },
      { label: 'Then', value: 'Which tool matches that job?' },
      { label: 'Compare', value: 'Helical vs push, grout vs foam' },
      { label: 'Not this page', value: 'A ranking of best products' },
    ]}
    nav={nav}
    topicsStyle="list"
  >
    <div class="ask-box" id="fitness">
      <h3>The right tool for the job</h3>
      <p>A method is only as good as the job it is asked to do. Helical piers, micropiles, push piers, grout, and foam are tools. A tool used on the wrong problem is the wrong tool, even when it is expensive or sounds more engineered.</p>
      <p>Sometimes the quieter option is the better one. Soil, water, access, structure weight, scope, and cost can make a simpler method the honest answer. The question is not which name sounds strongest. It is which method matches the condition that was actually found.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="questions">Questions this hub should leave you able to answer</h2>
    <p>Open a group. The answers live on this page and on the method pages the links point to.</p>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="q-what">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">What is this method?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner">
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/helical-piers">What is a helical pier?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/push-piers">What is a push pier?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/micropiles">What is a micropile?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/compaction-grouting">What is compaction grouting?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/foam-jacking">What is foam jacking?</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="q-diff">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">What is the difference?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner">
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#helical">Helical pier vs push pier?</a> Helical piers are rotated in with plates. Push piers are pressed from the foundation using the building as reaction.</li>
            <li><a href="/micropiles">Helical or micropile vs “just put steel in the ground”?</a> A product name is not a design. Access, vibration, load, and ground decide the tool.</li>
            <li><a href="#grout">Compaction grouting vs foam jacking?</a> Grout densifies soil. Foam fills accessible voids and can lift a slab. They are not the same material and not the same job.</li>
            <li><a href="#foam">A pier vs filling a void under a slab?</a> Underpinning a footing and filling a slab void are different load paths.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="q-when">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">When is this the right tool — and when is it not?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner">
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/compaction-grouting">When is compaction grouting most effective?</a> When the soil can be densified and the job is ground improvement, not a structural pile.</li>
            <li><a href="/foam-jacking">When is foam the wrong tool?</a> Active water, erosion, weak soil, or a footing that needs a pier — not a slab void fill.</li>
            <li><a href="/push-piers">When is a push pier a poor fit?</a> When the house is too light, the footing cannot take the reaction, or the cause of movement is still open.</li>
            <li><a href="/micropiles">When does limited access point to micropiles?</a> Tight working room, vibration limits, obstructions, or loads that another system cannot reach cleanly.</li>
            <li><a href="/all-articles/when-monitoring-makes-more-sense-than-immediate-underpinning">When is monitoring better than any of these?</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="q-cause">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Cause before product</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner">
          <ul>
            <li>Can drainage or a plumbing leak make a good method fail? Yes. Water that is still changing the soil is not fixed by steel or grout alone.</li>
            <li>Does a crack mean you need piers? No. A crack is a symptom. Seasonal movement and material behavior produce cracks on stable houses too.</li>
            <li>Stabilize vs lift — are those the same job? No. Holding a building where it is and raising it are different objectives.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="q-names">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Names that show up on bids</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner">
          <p>If the proposal says drilled shaft, caisson, CFA, H-pile, pipe pile, timber pile, sheet pile, or Geopier / rammed aggregate pier, start here:</p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="methods">The methods</h2>
    <div class="condition-grid">
      <div class="condition-card" id="helical">
        <h4>Helical Piers</h4>
        <p>Steel elements installed by rotating helical bearing plates into the ground. They transfer structural load to deeper soil or rock.</p>
        <p>They are not a universal answer to cracking, moisture change, drainage problems, voids, or every form of settlement.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/helical-piers">Helical Piers →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="micropiles">
        <h4>Micropiles</h4>
        <p>Small-diameter, drilled-and-grouted reinforced piles used for structural support and, in some work, in-place earth reinforcement.</p>
        <p>They are often considered where access, vibration, ground conditions, obstructions, or load make another approach a poor fit. They are not simply a more technical name for any pier repair.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/micropiles">Micropiles →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="push">
        <h4>Push Piers</h4>
        <p>Steel resistance piers hydraulically pressed from an existing foundation, using the building’s weight as the installation reaction.</p>
        <p>They are used for selected existing-building underpinning. They are not torque-installed helical piers, and they are not automatically appropriate when the structure is light, the footing is unsuitable, or the cause of movement is still unresolved.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/push-piers">Push Piers →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="grout">
        <h4>Compaction Grouting</h4>
        <p>A ground-improvement method that injects stiff, low-mobility grout to displace and densify suitable soil.</p>
        <p>It is used to improve support in specific ground conditions. It is not expanding polyurethane foam and it is not a conventional structural pile.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="foam">
        <h4>Foam Jacking</h4>
        <p>Expanding polyurethane injected beneath a slab to fill accessible voids and, where conditions allow, lift or re-level concrete.</p>
        <p>It is used for slab support and elevation correction. It is not pile underpinning and it is not a substitute for correcting an active water, erosion, or weak-soil condition.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="other">
        <h4>Other Deep Foundations</h4>
        <p>A catalog of names that often arise in commercial and infrastructure work: drilled shafts, auger-cast / CFA piles, driven H-piles and pipe piles, timber piles, precast concrete piles, caissons, sheet piling, and rammed aggregate piers.</p>
        <p>This is not a sixth residential repair product page. It explains systems owners may hear about and why their normal purpose may be different from repairing an existing house.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ask-box" id="before">
      <h3>Before any method is sold</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What movement or support problem is this method intended to solve?</li>
        <li>What investigation supports that explanation — site observations, elevation data, soil information, drainage review, records, monitoring, or engineering analysis?</li>
        <li>Is the goal stabilization, lifting, deep load transfer, soil improvement, void filling, retaining soil, or support for a new load?</li>
        <li>What is the load path from the building into the proposed system?</li>
        <li>What conditions make this method a better fit than the other relevant options?</li>
        <li>Were water, drainage, plumbing leaks, erosion, irrigation, utility trenches, and moisture-driven soil changes evaluated separately?</li>
        <li>What limitations, risks, and conditions for success are specific to this method and this site?</li>
        <li>What installation records, monitoring, testing, or verification will show whether the intended objective was achieved?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </HubPageShell>
</BaseLayout>
